@@ -495,8 +495,6 @@ A successful `click` or `press` means the action was sent to where the command f
 
 When an iOS runtime advertises native press readiness, a single primary `click` or `press` with a selector or `@ref` checks whether the resolved control can receive input. If a dismissing overlay still blocks it, the command waits within its existing request budget. An already-resolved target from a mutating `find` or replay is checked once and refuses a blocked control immediately. The target's identity, frame and app must stay the same while waiting. A missing native match refuses immediately; only a matched control that cannot yet receive input is waited on. This check does not prove that a partially covered control's center is clear. Raw coordinates and non-default tap options keep their existing behavior.
 
-For non-secure text inputs, readiness compares the captured value. A capture can report the placeholder for an empty field, so a field whose contents change to or from that same text can still match and be pressed once unblocked. Secure-field values are omitted; readiness cannot detect their content changes. Run `snapshot -i` and use a fresh selector to re-observe the target before retrying. Add `--verify` to check for a screen change after the press.
-
 A native-readiness refusal reports `COMMAND_FAILED`, `details.reason: target_not_ready`, and `details.dispatched: no`; `details.readiness` identifies the refusal or last observed state. An enclosing bounded batch or replay deadline can instead report `daemon_request_timeout`. A refused `@ref` press needs a fresh `snapshot -i` and a selector before retrying, because re-observation retires its ref frame. Cancellation retains the normal `request_canceled` reason and delivery evidence; cancellation after a request was sent can report `dispatched: unknown`. Typed native transport errors also keep their own error codes and reasons.
 
 A `click` or `press` whose target the daemon resolves to a point (`@ref` or selector) re-checks that target's position before dispatching when the session's previous action animated the screen, and dispatches where it came to rest. When that check cannot confirm a stable position — the target was still moving, or a re-check could not measure it — the command refuses with `target_still_moving` and `dispatched: no`: nothing was dispatched, the point the command resolved is in `error.details.rect`, and where the target was last seen is in `error.details.movedToRect` when that last look measured one. Run `wait <selector>` on the target and retry the same command, or add `--settle` so the action waits for the UI to go quiet. A refused `@ref` press needs a fresh `snapshot -i` and a selector, because the wait retires the ref. Two dispatch paths hand off before the check: a press at raw coordinates has no resolved target to re-check, and a dispatch the backend performs on the element itself — `click @ref` on a backend with element dispatch, or a Maestro non-hittable coordinate fallback — goes to that backend rather than through a resolved point.
